<SectionBlock
  id="difference"
  eyebrow="How this differs"
  title="Other frameworks ask whether an institution manages risk. These standards ask whether a challenge that succeeds changes the system."
  description="These standards work alongside the GDPR, the EU AI Act, the NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, and the OECD AI Principles. They specify what those leave open: who has to act when a running system is wrong, when its permission to act ends, and who carries the cost until then."
  variant="default"
>
  <p>
    Those frameworks improve documentation and oversight. They say less about
    what happens once a running system is wrong: whether someone
    halts it, reverses its decisions, and repairs the harm, and how long the
    person it is harming waits. They say least about whether anyone changes the
    rule that produced the error, or only fixes the one case.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a system cuts its own workload, the work it drops lands on someone:
    the nurse fixing a scheduler's mistakes, the claimant proving a denial
    wrong. That work is part of the system, so the person doing it has
    standing to challenge it. The cost belongs to the institution that runs
    the system, not to the people it serves.
  </p>
  <div class="mapping-table__wrapper">
    <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Existing standard says</th>
          <th scope="col">Ethotechnics requires</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says">
            "Contest the decision"<br />
            <span class="small muted">GDPR, Art. 22(3)</span>
          </td>
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">
            A revision or a reasoned refusal for every objection, on the
            record, within a set clock
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says">
            "Review the management system at planned intervals"<br />
            <span class="small muted">ISO/IEC 42001, cl. 9.3</span>
          </td>
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">
            A grant that expires unless someone renews it on evidence stated in
            advance, and a new authorization for every widening of scope
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says">
            "Characterize impacts on individuals and communities"<br />
            <span class="small muted">NIST AI RMF, MAP 5</span>
          </td>
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">
            A published measure of the time the system costs people, and
            standing for whoever absorbs its errors
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says">
            "Maintain human oversight"<br />
            <span class="small muted">EU AI Act, Art. 14</span>
          </td>
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">
            A named person with authority to halt it, a tested halt path, and
            a recovery deadline
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says">
            "Implement responsible AI principles"<br />
            <span class="small muted">OECD AI Principles</span>
          </td>
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">
            Binding escalation: a named owner, a deadline, and a set action, or
            the system falls back to a safe mode
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted small">
    Each requirement on the right is a test a running system passes or fails.
    A compliance document cannot pass it on its own. See the
    <a href="/standards/#comparison">full standards comparison</a> for the detailed
    analysis.
  </p>
</SectionBlock>
